    <h1>Depth-First Search (DFS)</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Go as far as you can down one path. At a dead end, step back and try another way.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>do DFS by hand with the <b>one-row method</b>, including every <b>backtrack</b>;</li>
        <li>give the DFS order (the <b>written</b> order) on directed and <b>disconnected</b> graphs, and the vertices never visited;</li>
        <li>compare BFS and DFS;</li>
        <li>match a Java DFS to the method.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/bfs/'}>BFS lesson</a>, the <a href={base + '/itc120/stacks/'}>Stacks lesson</a>, the <a href={base + '/itc120/recursion/'}>Recursion lesson</a>, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="idea">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>What DFS is and where it is used</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>What is DFS?</h3>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Depth-first search (DFS)</p>
        <p><b>DFS</b> is the second <Peek label="graph traversal" title="From the BFS lesson" html={traversalPeek} /> algorithm in this unit. It <span class="hl-mark">follows one path to its end</span> before it tries another path. At each vertex, it moves on to a neighbor that it has not visited yet.</p>
        <p>When it reaches a <b>dead end</b>, which is a vertex with no unvisited neighbors, it <b>backtracks</b>. This means it goes back one step and tries another neighbor there. DFS uses a <b>stack</b> to remember the way back.</p>
      </div>
      <h3>DFS in real life</h3>
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of looking for a lost file</p>
        <p>You saved your <span class="fname is-file">Lab 3</span> file, but you cannot find it. So you look for it like this.</p>
        <ol class="lost-steps">
          <li>Open <span class="fname">Documents</span>.</li>
          <li>Open <span class="fname">ITC120</span>.</li>
          <li>Open <span class="fname">Week 9</span>. The file is not there, and there are no folders inside, so there is nowhere deeper to go. Click <b>Back</b> to return to <span class="fname">ITC120</span>.</li>
          <li>Open <span class="fname">Week 10</span>. The file is here.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>You go deeper while you can, and you go back only when you are stuck.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={folderSvg} /><figcaption>Each column is one folder deeper. The numbers show the order the folders were opened. The Back button always returns you to the folder you came from, like a stack.</figcaption></figure>
      <h3>You already know DFS from trees</h3>
      <p>You have done a depth-first traversal before. The <Peek label="tree traversals" title="From the Traversals lesson" html={treePeek} /> called pre-order, in-order, and post-order go down one branch before coming back, so they are <b>depth-first</b>. DFS does the same thing on a graph. The difference is that a graph can have cycles, so DFS must remember which vertices it has already visited.</p>
      <h3>When DFS is used</h3>
      <p>Besides searching folders, DFS is used whenever each path must be explored all the way to its end. Here are two more examples.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={dfsUsesIT()} /><figcaption class="gi-cap">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Solving mazes.</b> A maze is a graph. The cat tries one way (1) until it reaches a dead end (2), backs up to the fork (3), and tries the next way (4).</li>
        <li><b>Finding cycles.</b> In a project plan, a cycle means that tasks wait for each other in a circle, so none of them can start. DFS follows the waits-for arrows. When it comes back to a task that is still on its path, it has found a cycle.</li>
      </ul>
      </figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="stack">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · The method</p>
      <h2>How to write the DFS traversal order</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>Recall the stack</h3>
      <p>Remember that a <b>stack</b> is also a behavior that can be built using an array or a linked list. This behavior is that whatever gets in last is the first one to get out, or the <b>LIFO</b> rule (last in, first out). Think of a stack of plates at the canteen. A clean plate is always put on top, and the next person takes the plate on top.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={canteenStack()} /><figcaption>Plate 5 was put on last, so it is taken first (<b>pop</b>). A new plate always goes on top (<b>push</b>).</figcaption></figure>
      <h3>Why DFS uses a stack</h3>
      <p>In DFS, the stack holds the <b>path you are on</b>. The <b>top</b> of the stack is the vertex where you are now. Only two things happen to it.</p>
      <ul class="push-pop">
        <li><b class="pp-push">Push.</b> When you move to a new neighbor, you put it on top of the stack. It becomes the new top, so that is where you are now.</li>
        <li><b class="pp-pop">Pop.</b> When you reach a dead end, you take it off the top. The vertex under it becomes the new top. That is the vertex you came from, so that is where you go back to.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <Peek label="lost-file example" title="From Part 1" html={backPeek} /> works the same way. The path bar <span class="fname">Documents</span> › <span class="fname">ITC120</span> › <span class="fname">Week 9</span> is a stack. Opening a folder adds it to the end (a push). Clicking <b>Back</b> removes the last folder (a pop), so you return to <span class="fname">ITC120</span>.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={stackToRow()} /><figcaption>On paper, we write the stack lying on its side, as one row. The top is the last letter on the right.</figcaption></figure>
      <h3>The one-row method</h3>
      <p>To find the DFS traversal order by hand, we write the stack as one row of vertices, the same way we wrote <Peek label="the queue in BFS" title="From the BFS lesson" html={bfsRowPeek} />. The difference is that we always work on the <b>last</b> letter without a check mark <span class="ck">✓</span>, which is the top of the stack. Go through the steps below.</p>
      <StepShow id="dfs-walk" cap="Finding the DFS traversal order" legend="dfs" steps={walkShow} />
      <p>Notice that the DFS order, <b>{walk.order.join(', ')}</b>, is different from the <Peek label="BFS order" title="From the BFS lesson" html={bfsRowPeek} />, {bfsWalkOrder.join(', ')}. BFS wrote C right after B, because both are one edge from A. DFS went deep through B and E first.</p>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Alphabetical order (same in BFS)</p>
        <p>A graph has no real first or last neighbor, so technically speaking, any choice of neighbor gives a correct DFS.</p>
        <p class="alpha-rule">But <b>in this course</b>, <span class="hl-mark">use alphabetical order</span> (smallest number first for numbers) so everyone gets the same answer. <span class="warn-txt">Quizzes and exams mark other orders wrong.</span></p>
      </div>
      <p class="small">DFS works the same way as <b>recursion</b>. Each call is a push, and each return is a pop. That is why DFS is usually written recursively (<a href="#java">Part 6</a>).</p>
      <details class="reveal"><summary>Extra: the "push-all" way in some books</summary><div class="flow">
        <p class="xtra-note"><b>Extra topic.</b> In this course, use the one-row method.</p>
        <p>Some books pop a vertex and, if it is not visited yet, visit it and push <b>all</b> its unvisited neighbors. That stack is a <b>to-do list</b>, not your path. The picture uses the <Peek label="DFS Example 1 graph" title="DFS Example 1" html={ex1Peek} />, right after DFS from A visits D.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={dfsTwoWays()} /><figcaption>Our stack is the path from A to D. The push-all stack holds D and E (already visited, so they are skipped when popped) and G twice.</figcaption></figure>
        <p class="after-fig">The order depends on which neighbor is pushed <b>last</b>, since it comes off first. A has two neighbors, B and D.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>If you push D, then B, you get the same order as ours, <span class="mono">A, B, C, F, E, D, G</span>.</li>
          <li>If you push B, then D, you get a different order, <span class="mono">A, D, E, G, F, C, B</span>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Some versions mark a vertex as visited when it is pushed. That can give an order that is not a real DFS.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="compare">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Side by side</p>
      <h2>BFS vs. DFS</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>BFS and DFS on the same graph</h3>
      <p>Both visit every reachable vertex once, but in a different order. Here is the DFS Example 1 graph searched both ways from A.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={bfsVsDfs()} /><figcaption>D is one edge from A, so BFS reaches it third. DFS reaches it sixth, at the end of a long path.</figcaption></figure>
      <h3>How BFS and DFS differ</h3>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>BFS</th><th>DFS</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Explores</b></td><td>wide first, level by level</td><td>deep first, then it backs up</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Uses a</b></td><td>queue (FIFO, first in, first out)</td><td>stack (LIFO, last in, first out), or recursion</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>On paper</b></td><td>Check the first letter without a ✓. Write all its new neighbors.</td><td>Work on the last letter without a ✓. Write only one new neighbor.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Fewest edges?</b></td><td>yes, in an unweighted graph</td><td>not always</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Good for</b></td><td>levels, fewest hops, friends of friends</td><td>every path, mazes, folders, cycles</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="remember" data-toc="How to remember BFS and DFS">
        <p class="note-label">How to remember BFS and DFS</p>
        <p><b>B</b>FS = <b>B</b>road (queue, waves). <b>D</b>FS = <b>D</b>eep (stack, backtrack).</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={rememberBfsDfs()} /></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="pieces">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Directed and disconnected graphs</p>
      <h2>DFS on directed and disconnected graphs</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>DFS on a directed graph</h3>
      <p>On a <b>directed</b> graph, DFS uses the same steps with one new rule (the same rule as in BFS), which is <span class="hl-mark">follow the arrows</span>. A vertex's neighbors are only the vertices its arrows point <b>to</b>. Here is the directed graph from the BFS lesson.</p>
      <StepShow id="dfs-dir" cap="Finding the DFS traversal order of a directed graph" legend="dfs" steps={dirShow} />
      <p>The DFS order is <b>{dirRun.order.join(', ')}</b>. The <Peek label="BFS order" title="From the BFS lesson" html={bfsDirPeek} /> on this graph is {bfsDirOrder.join(', ')}. DFS had to step back all the way to A before it could write D. F is <b>never visited</b>, because no arrow leads to F.</p>
      <h3>Disconnected graphs</h3>
      <p>Recall that a <Peek label="disconnected graph" title="From the Graphs lesson" html={disconnPeek} /> is still one graph, but some vertices cannot reach each other. Each separate piece is called a <b>component</b>.</p>
      <p>BFS and DFS only visit the <b>component where they start</b>. So "never visited" means every vertex outside that component. In the graph below, DFS from C visits only A, B, C, D, and E. F and G are never visited.</p>
      <figure class="fig">
        <Fragment set:html={pieceFig} />
        <p class="small">This is the DFS Example 2 graph. It has two components, and F with G is the one DFS from C never reaches.</p>
      </figure>
      <p class="small">To visit every vertex, a program starts DFS again from a vertex that is not visited yet.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="examples">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Practice problems</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="remember" data-toc="How your answers are checked">
        <p class="note-label">How your answers are checked</p>
        <p>The stack row with check marks <span class="ck">✓</span> is <b>scratch work</b>, and it is <b>not graded</b>. You hand in these answers.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>a.</b> The <span class="hl-mark">DFS traversal order</span>.</li>
          <li><b>b.</b> <span class="hl-mark">Other questions</span>, such as which vertices are never visited.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>At each step, ask two questions. What is the last letter without a ✓? Does it have a neighbor that is not written yet? Do each example on paper first, then check it step by step.</p>
      {ex.map((e) => (
        <div class="paper flow ex-q" id={e.id} data-toc={e.title.split(':')[0]}>
          <p class="ex-title"><span class="ex-tag">{e.title.split(': ')[0]}</span>{e.title.includes(': ') && <span class="ex-sub">{e.title.split(': ')[1].replace(/^./, (c) => c.toUpperCase())}</span>}</p>
          <p class="ex-task">Perform a DFS traversal with <b>{e.start}</b> as the starting point (neighbors in alphabetical order).</p>
          <GraphFig g={e.g} label={`${e.title} graph`} />
          <ol class="ex-parts">
            <li><b>a.</b> What is the DFS traversal order?</li>
            {e.missedQ && <li><b>b.</b> {e.missedQ}</li>}
          </ol>
          <details class="reveal">
            <summary>Show the solution, step by step</summary>
            <div class="flow">
              <StepShow id={`${e.id}-steps`} cap={`DFS from ${e.start}`} legend="dfs" steps={e.show} />
              <div class="ex-ans">
                <p class="ex-a-line"><b class="lbl">a.</b> <span class="ans">{e.run.order.join(', ')}</span></p>
                {e.missedQ && <p class="ex-a-line"><b class="lbl">b.</b> <span class="ans">{and(e.run.missed)}</span></p>}
              </div>
              {e.peekNote
                ? <p>The answer starts with C, the start. F and G are a <Peek label="separate component" title="From Part 4" html={piecePeek} />, so they are never visited.</p>
                : <p set:html={e.note} />}
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      ))}

      <div class="paper flow ex-q" id={flood.id} data-toc="Real-life Example 2">
        <p class="ex-title"><span class="ex-tag">Real-life Example 2</span><span class="ex-sub">Rescue after a typhoon</span></p>
        <p>This is the same <Peek label="sample town" title="Real-life Example 1" html={realPeek} /> as in the BFS lesson. Two roads are flooded (✗), so treat them as gone. A rescue truck leaves the Fire station. Which places are cut off?</p>
        <p class="ex-task">Do DFS from the Fire station (<b>F</b>), neighbors in alphabetical order. Ignore the flooded roads.</p>
        <div class="town-fig"><GraphFig g={W.townFlood} labels={townNames} w={380} label="Sample town with two flooded roads: Market to Plaza, and School to Library" /></div>
        <ol class="ex-parts">
          <li><b>a.</b> What is the DFS traversal order?</li>
          <li><b>b.</b> Which places are cut off?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Show the solution, step by step</summary>
          <div class="flow">
            <StepShow id="real-ex2-steps" cap="DFS from F (Fire station)" legend="dfs" steps={flood.show} />
            <p class="small">The red ✗ marks a flooded road.</p>
            <div class="ex-ans">
              <p class="ex-a-line"><b class="lbl">a.</b> <span class="ans">{flood.run.order.join(', ')}</span></p>
              <p class="ex-a-line"><b class="lbl">b.</b> <span class="ans">{and(cutNames)}</span></p>
            </div>
            <p>The floods split the town into two components. The truck can only reach its own component. The other one needs a boat. (BFS would reach the same places, in a different order.)</p>
            <p class="small">Network tools use this to see what is still connected after a failure, such as a broken cable or a fallen power line.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · In code</p>
      <h2>DFS in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>The DFS program</h3>
      <p>DFS is short when it is written with recursion. This program builds the graph of <Peek label="DFS Example 1" title="DFS Example 1" html={ex1Peek} /> as an adjacency matrix, then runs DFS from A.</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(code)} /></pre>
      <TryIt cls="DFS" />
      <h3>Reading the code aloud</h3>
      <p>The matrix lines (<code>names</code>, <code>adj</code>, <code>addEdge</code>) are read the same way as in the <Peek label="Graphs lesson" title="Reading the matrix lines aloud" html={matrixPeek} />. Here are the three new lines to read aloud.</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl ra">
        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>static boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];</code></td><td>"make n true/false slots named visited, all <code>false</code> at first." It is <code>static</code> (outside the method), so every recursive call of <code>dfs</code> shares the same array.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>adj[u][v] == 1 &amp;&amp; !visited[v]</code></td><td>"v is a neighbor of u <b>and</b> v is <b>not</b> visited yet."</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>dfs(v);</code></td><td>"go deeper." It runs <code>dfs</code> on v all the way, then comes back and continues this loop.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Match the code to the one-row method</p>
        <p><code>visited[u] = true</code> means "u is now written in the row". Calling <code>dfs(v)</code> is writing one new neighbor and moving to it (a push). When the <code>for</code> loop ends, no neighbor is left that is not written, so the method returns. That is writing the ✓ and stepping back (a pop). There is no stack variable here, because Java's call stack is the stack.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow" id="task" data-toc="Your coding task">
        <p class="note-label">Your coding task (count what DFS can reach)</p>
        <p>In <Peek label="this one-way graph" title="A one-way (directed) graph" html={reachPeek} />, DFS from A reaches 5 vertices, but DFS from F reaches only 3. Now write a DFS that counts them.</p>
        <p>Finish <code>countReach</code> in about 8 lines where it says YOUR CODE. It is the <code>dfs</code> method from this part with a count added. Each call counts its own vertex (1), plus everything its deeper calls reach. Like <Peek label="height in the Recursion lesson" title="height, from the Recursion lesson" html={heightPeek} />, a recursive call gives back a number, and you add it. The graph is the one-way graph, built with <code>addArrow</code> (one direction only). <code>main</code> clears <code>visited</code> before each new test. Run it, and check that each printed line matches its expected comment.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show one possible answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskAns)} /></pre>
          <p>The output, one per line, is <code>5</code>, <code>3</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>5</code>.</p>
          <p>Here is the first test, from A. The deepest calls finish first.</p>
          <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Why</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(C)</code></td><td>C's only neighbor, A, is already visited</td><td>1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(B)</code></td><td>B itself + C</td><td>1 + 1 = 2</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(E)</code></td><td>no arrows go out of E</td><td>1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(D)</code></td><td>D itself + E</td><td>1 + 1 = 2</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="nowrap"><code>countReach(A)</code></td><td>A itself + B's 2 + D's 2</td><td>1 + 2 + 2 = 5</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>The last test starts at B and also reaches 5. It goes B → C → A, then A → D → E. The arrow C → A leads back to A. Only F is missed, because no arrow points into F.</p>
          <p>Here are two common mistakes.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Writing only <code>countReach(v);</code>. The call still runs, but the number it gives back is thrown away. Every call returns 1, so every test prints <code>1</code>.</li>
            <li>Forgetting <code>visited[u] = true</code>. Then DFS goes A → B → C → A → B → … around the cycle forever. Each call waits for the next one, and Java's call stack fills up, so the program stops with <code>StackOverflowError</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>Quiz</h3>
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-dfs" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember" data-toc="Key takeaways">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li>DFS follows one path to its end, then backtracks at dead ends. It uses a stack (or recursion).</li>
          <li>On paper, write the stack as one row. You are always on the <b>last</b> letter without a ✓.</li>
          <li>If you are not stuck, write <b>one</b> new neighbor (the first alphabetically) and move to it. If you are stuck, write a ✓ and step back. Repeat until every letter has a ✓.</li>
          <li>The DFS order is the order the letters were <b>written</b>, not the order they were checked.</li>
          <li>In a directed graph, follow the arrows only. BFS and DFS only visit the component where they start.</li>
          <li>BFS is broad, uses a queue, and finds the fewest edges. DFS is deep, uses a stack, and follows one path to its end before it tries another.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next unit:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/sets/'}>Sets in Java</a>.</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest, and C. Stein, <i>Introduction to Algorithms</i>, 4th ed. MIT Press, 2022, Section 20.3 (Depth-first search).</li>
          <li>Programiz, "Depth First Search (DFS) Algorithm." <a href="https://www.programiz.com/dsa/graph-dfs">programiz.com/dsa/graph-dfs</a></li>
          <li>A. Bhargava, <i>Grokking Algorithms</i>. Manning, 2016, Chapter 6 (graphs and BFS, for comparison).</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
